README: describe draco in draco's terms, and name what is still Scala's

Second of the documentation passes, after DRACO.md. Two jobs: correct the drift, and take
the target language out of the architecture description.

THE DRIFT. README claimed four aspects (five), a `values` field and flat variables on
RuleAspect (pattern carries variables and conditions; values retired), rule definitions in
`.rule.json` files projected to `…Rule`-suffixed types (bare, both), `Generator.loadType`
and `loadRuleType` (TypeLoader owns loading, one path), a `draco.language` domain and
YAML authoring with `from-yaml`/`to-yaml` (all removed), five-way dispatch (six), "there is
no draco-endogenous actor" (the Draco domain carries one and validates its own dictionary
with it), `TypeElement` over `Primal[String]` with ten kinds (Primal[Json], eleven — Local
was missing), releases through alpha.3 (alpha.5), and a project tree with no .drake, no
format/rete/drake/generator/scalatarget, and a `language/` that does not exist. Nothing in
it mentioned DRAKE at all: 91 surface files, an emitter, a parser, and two of the three
gates.

THE VOCABULARY, which is the substantive change. Draco is meant to project into several
languages, so an architecture written in one target's terms describes the current
projection rather than the framework. The architecture sections now say type, derivation,
element, globals, factory, modules, aspect, projection — and `[T]` `{T}` `{K,V}` `(A, B)`
`A -> B` for the type constructors. A short table up front gives each neutral term against
the host term it replaces, so the substitution is legible rather than merely performed.
Verified after writing: `trait`, `extends`, `sealed` and `Map[` survive in the architecture
half only inside that table's "rather than" column, where they are the terms being
replaced.

The parameter notation follows: `Primal(T)`, `Holon((S, T))`, `Domain(T)` — DRAKE's
application form, not the host's brackets.

AND A LANGUAGE-SPECIFIC RESIDUES SECTION, which is the part meant to do work. Some host
vocabulary cannot be removed yet, and scattering apologies through the prose would hide how
much there is. Eight leaks are tabulated in one place — initialization discipline, the
sequence and set spellings still in valueTypes, host-opaque value strings, the codec
realization, the rule-evaluation and actor-behaviour bindings, structural identity authored
as host-named members, and type parameters encoded as strings so that abstractness is not
computable — each with what neutral would look like. The list is meant to shrink, and the
map constructor is named as the worked example of the move: `{K, V}` normative in both
definition and surface, `Map[K, V]` produced in exactly one function in the target.

That framing also let the abstract/concrete rule be stated properly for the first time: a
parameter position holds a variable or a concrete type, a type is abstract iff any position
holds a variable at any depth, a constraint never makes a position concrete, and only a
fully concrete name can be the derivation for an atomic term — which is why `Meters`
deriving `Distance(Double)` is a term and `Dictionary(K, V)` is not.

The operational sections — building, getting started, dependencies, project structure — stay
Scala-specific and now say so explicitly at the boundary. They describe this repository's
realization, not the architecture.

NOT DONE. GETTING_STARTED.md and CHANGELOG.md remain; AGENTS.md is still a diverged copy
rather than a symlink and deleting tracked content is Dev's call.
